diniViewport.sh`, prints the mean and std of the viewport), change ONE thing through the bridge (delegate, device, lights bypassed, bare sphere, display flag moved node by node), wait 15 to 20 s, capture again. Flipbook cannot capture a Hydra Cycles viewport (always white).\n- 6 October 2026 : what did NOT matter: the Metal or CPU device (`CYCLES_DEVICE`), the lights (bypassing them did not change the white), the scene index variable (set with `launchctl setenv USDIMAGINGGL_ENGINE_ENABLE_SCENE_INDEX 0` for the session, resets on reboot, not proven necessary for the viewport). Octane viewport: dark image (mean 69), not examined further.\n- 6 October 2026 : the Cycles viewport still shows pale petals and a lit side only: lights for Cycles are dome 0.17 and exposure -1.5, key exposure 0.4 (Karma values: dome 1.0 and 0.0, key 1.5).\n\n## 6 October 2026 : HOW to get a good looking Cycles viewport in Solaris (recipe, step by step)\n\n- 6 October 2026 : HOW THE WHITE SCREEN WAS SOLVED (repeat of the fix, for a quick find): (1) switch the viewport to Cycles (the label at the top of the viewport, \"Cycles Persp\"); (2) capture the Houdini window with `screencapture -l <windowid>` (`Scripts/CaptureHoudiniViewport.sh`) to SEE the viewport, a flipbook cannot; (3) test a bare sphere in `/stage`: it rendered, so the engine was fine; (4) put the display flag on the nodes of the chain ONE AT A TIME (`lt_assign`, `lt_dome`, `lt_key`, `lt_karma`): the viewport went white only with the display flag on `lt_karma` (the Karma Render Settings LOP); (5) leave the display flag on `lt_key` (or any node BEFORE `lt_karma`); `lt_render` still renders through its LOP Path `/stage/lt_karma`.\n- 6 October 2026 : WHY THE CYCLES VIEW LOOKED UGLY AND HOW IT WAS FIXED. (a) Cycles reads the material PREVIEW network (`UsdPreviewSurface`), not the Karma Principled shader. The preview normal map, read the wrong way, drew black fibres on the petals and black patches on the leaves: a Python LOP `lt_cycles_preview_fix` (placed after `lt_assign`, before `lt_camera`) clears the `inputs:normal` connection of every UsdPreviewSurface; Karma keeps its own normal map. (b) Lights: Cycles is much more sensitive than Karma and the dome is the VISIBLE background, so the dome sets the background grey: dome intensity 0.25 and exposure -1.3 gives a mid grey; key light exposure -0.8 with angle 6 (soft shadows); a FILL light `lt_fill` (copy of the key, rotation y + 160, x -15, exposure -1.9) lights the sides in shadow. Karma values to restore for Karma: dome 1.0 / 0, key 1.5.\n- 6 October 2026 : WHERE the Cycles settings are. There is NO Cycles node in Solaris (searched all node types for \"cycles\"): the delegate is configured by attributes on the render settings prim, `cycles:samples`, `cycles:device`, `cycles:threads`, `cycles:time_limit`, `cycles:integrator:*` (max_bounce and others, `use_denoise`, `use_adaptive_sampling`, `use_light_tree`); the viewport quality is also set from the viewport's own render options (the small pencil icon next to the \"Cycles Persp\" label). To author them in the graph: an Edit Properties or Python LOP that sets the attribute on `/Render/rendersettings`. Not done yet: the display flag must stay BEFORE `lt_karma`, so such a node would have to sit before it.\n- 6 October 2026 : what is still different from Karma: Cycles has no Karma Principled features (no sheen, no translucency from the Principled), the petals are paler, the lighting is plain. A custom Cycles material (MaterialX or Cycles shader network) would be the next step for a real match.\n\n## 6 October 2026 : Cycles lookdev is now driven by an editable file\n\n- 6 October 2026 (Matthieu: \"tenir dans le skill une section à jour avec les paramètres que je peux éditer\"): `CyclesLookdev1.md` next to this file holds a JSON block (dome, key, fill, normal map on or off, viewport delegate, display node) with a table that explains each value. Matthieu edits it, says \"applique le lookdev Cycles\", and